 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436122 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436122 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436122 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f486c765-ecd7-5a87-8f26-635e2f270bec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1071 "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436123 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436124 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436125 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436126 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436127 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436128 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
